{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["And the reason for <b>to the priest who shall be in those days</b> — to the priest who shall be there in those days — is that they are given to the men of the watch, like all offerings; it does not mean that he should bring with him a priest from his own city to offer his first fruits. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 26:3): \"You have none other than the priest who shall be in your days, as he is.\" And I do not understand this, for regarding the matter of the elder it is fitting to say, <b>to the judge who shall be in those days</b> (Deuteronomy 17:9) — even though he is not as great and wise as the earlier judges who preceded us in the world, one must listen to him; let Jephthah in his generation be like Samuel in his generation. But in the bringing of first fruits, to whom would one bring them if not to the priest who shall be in his days? And so I found in the Sifrei (Tavo 3): \"And you shall come to the priest who shall be\" — this is what Rabbi Yosei HaGlili says: would it occur to you that it means a priest who is not in your days? Rather, a priest who is established and qualified for you on those days. If he was nearby and then moved far away, he is qualified. And so it says (Ecclesiastes 7:10): Do not say, what was — that the former days were better than these, etc. And it appears that they derive from this that if one offered first fruits, or a burnt offering and other offerings, through a priest who is under the presumption of qualification, and after some time he is found to be the son of a divorced woman, the offerings are valid, as mentioned in the last chapter of Kiddushin (66a). And it further enters into this that if he was nearby and then moved far away, he is qualified — and this pertains to the matter of the elder, who is not judged on the basis of proximity, and is not a relevant matter for first fruits:" "<b>I have declared this day</b> — regarding this fruit that I have brought, I have declared and given thanks to HaShem your Elohim that He brought me into the land that He swore to our fathers to give us, and behold HaShem fulfills His words and I give thanks and praise to His name. And the meaning of הִגַּדְתִּי is like סִפַּרְתִּי (I have recounted), as in וַיַּגֵּד מֹשֶׁה אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָעָם אֶל ה' (Exodus 19:9) — a recounting. Or its meaning may be: I have declared to you, the priest, and to all who stand here, and this declaration is to HaShem your Elohim — that is, to His name — for I have come into the land that He swore to our fathers, for He brought me to it, to serve Him in the land."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The reference to Kiddushin 66a concerns a priest discovered to be a ben gerushah (son of a divorced woman); Ramban applies the Sifrei's phrase קרוב ונתרחק to two distinct halakhic contexts."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:31.736310204Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}